# PRISM backend configuration.
#
# Copy this file to backend/.env and fill in the key:
#
#   Windows   copy backend\.env.example backend\.env
#   macOS/Linux   cp backend/.env.example backend/.env
#
# .env is read at startup and stays server-side. The browser never sees the key,
# and /api/health reports only whether one is present.

# --- Required ----------------------------------------------------------------

# Get one at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
# Without it the API still starts, /api/health answers key_configured: false,
# and POST /api/proposals returns 503 with an actionable message.
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# --- Optional ----------------------------------------------------------------

# Verified model ids: gemini-3.5-flash (default), gemini-3.1-pro-preview,
# gemini-2.5-pro, gemini-2.5-flash, gemini-pro-latest, gemini-flash-latest.
# Flash is the right default here: one call, structured output, images.
GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash

# --- Database ----------------------------------------------------------------

# Local development needs no setting: PRISM uses SQLite at
# backend/generated/prism.db. Production should set a PostgreSQL URL; Docker
# does this automatically for its private postgres service.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://prism:password@localhost:5432/prism

# Where the local SQLite file and generated/uploaded assets live. Relative
# paths resolve against backend/.
# GENERATED_DIR=generated

# Comma-separated browser origins allowed to call the API. The default covers
# the Vite dev server on both hostnames.
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5174,http://127.0.0.1:5174

# Bind address and port for `py -m app.main`.
# HOST=127.0.0.1
# PORT=8000

# Ask the model whether a brief describes work at all, before pricing it.
# One small call at the start of every generation. Set to 0 to switch it off
# and let every brief through - the structural floor (empty, too short,
# placeholder text) still applies either way, because that costs nothing.
# CHECK_BRIEF_IS_REAL=1

# Upload and input caps.
# MAX_IMAGES=8
# MAX_IMAGE_BYTES=8388608
# MAX_BRIEF_CHARS=20000

# --- Accounts (optional) -----------------------------------------------------
#
# Sign-in: PRISM's own screen, with Zitadel (https://zitadel.com) keeping the
# people - their passwords, whether their email is proven, which Google or
# Facebook account is theirs - and sending every code by email. The API checks
# what the person typed with Zitadel, then issues PRISM's own tokens.
#
# Leave all of these empty and PRISM works exactly as it always has: no sign-in
# screen, and an API that answers anyone who can reach it. Set them and the API
# verifies a token on every call - a browser without a session gets 401, and so
# does curl. Set only some and every call is refused until the rest are set.
#
# To set it up against a Zitadel instance (zitadel.neptune.ph is PRISM's own;
# docs: /var/www/repos/zitadel/README.md on the Neptune server):
#   1. An organization for PRISM's people. Its id is ZITADEL_ORG_ID; new
#      accounts are made there and every lookup is limited to it.
#   2. A machine user in it with the instance role IAM_LOGIN_CLIENT, and a
#      personal access token for it. That token is ZITADEL_TOKEN. It can start a
#      sign-in for anybody: keep it as secret as a database password.
#   3. An active SMTP provider (Default Settings -> SMTP Provider), because every
#      code goes out by email.
#   4. Default Settings -> Secret Generators: "Email verification" and "One-time
#      password by email" as 6 digits. The sign-in screen draws six digit boxes.
#   5. AUTH_TOKEN_SECRET: 32+ random characters (`openssl rand -hex 32`). It
#      signs PRISM's own tokens; changing it signs everybody out.
#   6. Google / Facebook: add them in the Zitadel console under the PRISM
#      organization (Settings -> Identity Providers) with the client id and
#      secret, and register the callback URL the console shows with Google or
#      Meta. Nothing to set here - the sign-in screen shows a button for each
#      provider Zitadel reports as active, and none for the rest.

# ZITADEL_URL=https://zitadel.neptune.ph
# ZITADEL_TOKEN=
# ZITADEL_ORG_ID=
# AUTH_TOKEN_SECRET=
# Another address for the same instance, reached from this server only. Empty
# means ZITADEL_URL is called.
# ZITADEL_INTERNAL_URL=

# --- Invitations (optional) --------------------------------------------------
#
# The one email PRISM sends: "you have been invited to this workspace". Without
# a key the invite is still created and the app shows you the link to send
# yourself. RESEND_FROM has to be on a domain verified in Resend.

# RESEND_API_KEY=re_...
# RESEND_FROM=invites@yourdomain.com

# Where client links and invitation links point - your app's origin, not the
# API's. The dev server pins itself to 5174 (`strictPort: true` in
# vite.config.ts), so 5174 is the only value that can be right in development.
# APP_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5174
